\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Airy intensity pattern for a uniformly illuminated, unobscured circular pupil with no aberration. Coordinates are measured in units of $\lambda /\mathrm  {NA}$, and intensity is normalized to its on-axis value. (a) The image-plane pattern uses a logarithmic colour scale to reveal the faint rings; colour does not represent wavelength. (b) The radial intensity on a linear scale shows the dominant central spot. The dashed line marks the first dark ring at $r=0.6098\lambda /\mathrm  {NA}$. (c) The same radial profile on a logarithmic scale reveals the outer rings. The first bright ring reaches only about $1.75$ percent of the central peak. The logarithmic displays stop at $I/I(0)=10^{-5}$; the ideal dark-ring minima are zero.}}{17}{figure.90}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:airy-pattern}{{4.1}{17}{Airy intensity pattern for a uniformly illuminated, unobscured circular pupil with no aberration. Coordinates are measured in units of $\lambda /\NA $, and intensity is normalized to its on-axis value. (a) The image-plane pattern uses a logarithmic colour scale to reveal the faint rings; colour does not represent wavelength. (b) The radial intensity on a linear scale shows the dominant central spot. The dashed line marks the first dark ring at $r=0.6098\lambda /\NA $. (c) The same radial profile on a logarithmic scale reveals the outer rings. The first bright ring reaches only about $1.75$ percent of the central peak. The logarithmic displays stop at $I/I(0)=10^{-5}$; the ideal dark-ring minima are zero}{figure.90}{}}
